<header id="problem">
<h1>AnaQorgau: protecting mothers after the delivery room</h1>
<p class="lede">Kazakhstan has cut maternal deaths and almost every woman gives birth in a facility. Yet most women who still die do so after delivery, some at home, and the risk differs sharply by region. The national average tells a story of success. The regional data tells a more complicated one.</p>

<h2>The issue and its scope</h2>
<p>The national maternal mortality ratio was 11.4 per 100,000 live births in 2023 (45 deaths) and 10.1 in 2024 (37 deaths). A fall like this invites the conclusion that the problem is solved. But in 2024 the ratio was 36.5 in Zhetisu, 34.1 in Kostanay, 29.6 in Ulytau and 27.9 in Aktobe, roughly three times the national figure, while three regions recorded no maternal death at all (Zakharyanova et al., 2026).</p>
<div class="facts">
<div class="fact"><b>70%</b><span>of 2024 deaths were postpartum (26 of 37), down from 76% in 2023</span></div>
<div class="fact"><b>22%</b><span>occurred at home, up from 16% in 2023</span></div>
<div class="fact"><b>57%</b><span>had indirect causes such as heart disease</span></div>
<div class="fact"><b>1:2,500</b><span>rural nurse or midwife to residents at some posts</span></div>
</div>
<p>The trend is mixed. Direct obstetric deaths fell from 23 to 16 and hemorrhage deaths from seven to three, but indirect deaths barely moved (22 to 21) and preeclampsia or eclampsia deaths rose from three to four. Rural infrastructure is also thinning: outpatient and inpatient facility density fell between 2004 and 2022 and is projected to fall further (Jobalayeva et al., 2025). A shortage of about 3,800 mid-level health workers compounds this (Alaidarova et al., 2026).</p>

<h2>The social science question inside it</h2>
<div class="q">Why do women in a middle-income state with near-universal facility births still die postpartum and at home, and whose risk is the health system organised around?</div>
<p>Thaddeus and Maine's (1994) Three Delays model gives the frame. Kazakhstan largely solved the third delay, adequate care once at a facility, in its hospitals. The first delay (deciding to seek care) and second (reaching it) persist where a single feldsher-midwife post covers a wide area, where young or low-income mothers start antenatal care late, and where early discharge sends women back to domestic and farm work with warning signs unnoticed. Institutions measure what happens in wards. The danger now lies in the weeks after.</p>
<p>Some women are not in the system at all. Seasonal migrants and residents of peri-urban settlements without a clinic registration (prikreplenie) fall outside automated Damumed antenatal tracking. These are the women a national average cannot see.</p>
</header>

<h2 id="data">Background and data</h2>
<h3>Regional ratios are unstable, and that is the finding</h3>
<p>The open-data portal publishes regional maternal mortality for two periods. Plotting every region shows something a single-year ranking hides: the high-risk list changes. Only Zhetisu and Ulytau are high in both periods.</p>
